# Tutorial 06 — Audio and Music
# How to play background music, trigger sound effects, and control audio fading.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_06_audio
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Close your eyes for a second. Imagine a scene with no sound at all.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Flat, right? Audio is half the experience in a visual novel — music sets the mood, sound effects sell the moment.
Lavender: Let's learn how to control it.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to explore?
> Background music (BGM) -> section_bgm
> Sound effects (SFX) -> section_sfx
> Fading and crossfading audio -> section_fading
> Voice lines -> section_voice
> Asset organization for audio -> section_organization
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Background Music ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_bgm
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[bgm]{/b} starts a music track that loops continuously in the background.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm "assets/audio/bgm/peaceful_morning.mp3"]{/color}
Lavender: That's it — one line and the music plays. It'll keep looping until you change it or stop it.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: To stop music:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_stop]{/color}
Lavender: To switch to a different track, just call [bgm] again with a new file. The old one stops automatically.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Music is the emotional backbone of every scene. A cheerful track makes the same dialogue feel completely different from a tense one.
Lavender: Choose your tracks carefully — they do more storytelling than you might think.
> Next: Sound effects -> section_sfx
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Sound Effects ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_sfx
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: {b}[sfx]{/b} plays a one-shot sound — a door closing, a phone buzzing, footsteps, thunder.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[sfx "assets/audio/sfx/door_close.wav"]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Unlike BGM, sound effects play once and stop. They don't loop.
Lavender: Time them right before or after a dialogue line for maximum impact:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[sfx "assets/audio/sfx/glass_break.wav"]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: What was that?!{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The sound plays, then the line appears. The player hears the crash {i}before{/i} the reaction — that's good pacing.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Keep SFX files short — half a second to two seconds is the sweet spot for most effects.
> Next: Fading -> section_fading
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Fading ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_fading
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Abruptly stopping music is jarring. Fading out is almost always better.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadeout 1200]{/color}
Lavender: That fades the current track to silence over 1200 milliseconds — about one second.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You can also fade {i}into{/i} a new track:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadein "assets/audio/bgm/tense_night.mp3" 800]{/color}
Lavender: The new track starts silent and rises to full volume over 800ms.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: For a smooth crossfade — old track out, new track in simultaneously:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadeout 1000]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[bgm_fadein "new_track.mp3" 1000]{/color}
Lavender: The durations overlap, so you get a seamless blend between the two tracks.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Fade durations are subjective, but 800–1500ms works for most situations. Shorter feels urgent, longer feels dreamy.
> Next: Voice -> section_voice
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Voice Lines ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_voice
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: If your project has voice acting, the {b}[voice]{/b} command plays a voice clip alongside a dialogue line.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[voice "assets/audio/voice/lavender_001.wav"]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I can't believe it's already sunset.{/color}
Lavender: The voice clip plays when the line appears. If the player clicks before it finishes, the clip stops.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Voice acting is optional — many great VNs are text-only. But if you add it, a few tips:
Lavender: Name files by character and line number: {b}lavender_001.wav{/b}, {b}alex_042.wav{/b}.
Lavender: Keep a separate {b}assets/audio/voice/{/b} folder. Voice files add up fast.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: And always give the player a volume slider for voice — some people love it, some prefer reading in silence.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Organization ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_organization
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Audio files multiply quickly. Here's a folder structure that keeps things sane:
narrator: {b}assets/audio/bgm/{/b} — background music tracks.
narrator: {b}assets/audio/sfx/{/b} — sound effects.
narrator: {b}assets/audio/voice/{/b} — voice lines, organized by character.
narrator: {b}assets/audio/ambience/{/b} — looping environmental sounds (rain, crowd noise, wind).
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: For file names, be descriptive: {b}cafe_jazz_loop.mp3{/b} beats {b}track_03.mp3{/b} every time.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: And use OGG or MP3 for music, WAV for short SFX. Smaller files load faster.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Audio cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}[bgm "path"]{/b} — play looping background music.
narrator: {b}[bgm_stop]{/b} — stop music immediately.
narrator: {b}[bgm_fadeout N]{/b} — fade current music to silence over N ms.
narrator: {b}[bgm_fadein "path" N]{/b} — start new track, fading in over N ms.
narrator: {b}[sfx "path"]{/b} — play a one-shot sound effect.
narrator: {b}[voice "path"]{/b} — play a voice clip with the next dialogue line.
narrator: {b}Folders:{/b} assets/audio/bgm/, sfx/, voice/, ambience/.
narrator: {b}Formats:{/b} MP3 or OGG for music, WAV for short SFX.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Audio is invisible when done well and painful when done wrong. Take the time to get it right.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's hear audio in action. Pick a mood:
> A peaceful morning -> practice_peaceful
> A spooky night -> practice_spooky
> A busy city -> practice_city

@label practice_peaceful
{{BGM_START}}
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: Birdsong filtered through the open window. The morning was already warm.
Lavender: Mmm. This is nice. The music does half the work here.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Notice how a calm BGM track completely changes how you read the narrator's description.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_spooky
[show lavender center talking]
narrator: The floorboards creaked under each step. Somewhere above, something scratched at the ceiling.
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: ...did you hear that? I {i}definitely{/i} heard something.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Even without actual audio files, the script structure is there — you'd add [bgm] and [sfx] right before these lines.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_city
[show lavender center happy]
narrator: Car horns, distant conversation, the rumble of a train passing underground — the city was alive.
Lavender: I love this kind of energy. Ambience tracks are perfect here — layer them under a light BGM.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The trick is keeping BGM volume lower when there's a lot of ambient sound. Don't fight for the player's ears.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
{{BGM_FADE}}
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Peaceful scenes pair best with soft, looping tracks. Let the music breathe — don't fill every second.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Horror relies heavily on audio. Silence is terrifying. A single SFX in an otherwise quiet scene is powerful.
[else]
Lavender: Busy environments need layered audio. BGM plus ambience creates a rich soundscape without overwhelming the dialogue.
[endif]
Lavender: Drop your own audio files into the assets folder and try wiring them up. You'll hear the difference immediately.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
